  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831125",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-141539",
      "knowledge_point_id": "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression",
      "problem": "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=3,\\,q=-4",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=6,\\,q=8",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=3,\\,q=5",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=6,\\,q=7",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=3,\\,q=-5",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
