  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831125",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-141622",
      "knowledge_point_id": "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term",
      "problem": "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
